  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 302 AM EDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\n=== Warm and Dry Today ===\n\nHigh pressure is pushing off to the east this morning, which is \nallowing for very light winds to veer to the southeast. Mild dry air \nmix down is expected again today, which will bring dew points into \nthe low 40s (for most) and high temperatures into the low-mid 80s. \nVented moisture from Hurricane Isaias is being carried north along \nthe 250mb flow. This will steadily bring scattered upper sky cover \nnorth over the region in the afternoon and evening.\n\n=== Remnants of Hurricane Isaias: Rainfall and Gusty Winds ===\n\nHurricane Isaias is currently a Category 2 with max winds of 105mph. \nThe system is expected to intensify to high-end Cat 2 or low-end Cat \n3 prior to landfall. Given the fair amount of shear, there is \ncurrently a low chance of the system intensifying beyond a low-end \nCat 3. This brings more confidence to the forecast over the lower \nOhio Valley as the system continues north after making landfall near \nthe western Florida panhandle tonight or very early Saturday \nmorning. \n\nOvercast skies will continue to work north over the lower Ohio \nValley tonight. Gradient winds and precip chances will steadily \nincrease Saturday mid-morning and into the early afternoon from \nsouth to north. Over the last 24 hours, the forecast has sped up by \nabout 6-12 hours. Expecting to see max winds of 30-35mph over \nsouthern Kentucky, and wouldn't completely rule out up to 40mph in \nsome more wind exposed areas. Northern Kentucky and southern Indiana \nwill see up to 30mph winds. Given a stable layer at the surface and \nantecedent overcast skies, CAPE continues to be very minimal during \nthis event. This decreases chances for tropical tornadoes. Though, \nif we see any breaks in the clouds in the afternoon on Saturday, we \ncould see locally enhanced wind gusts and a small chance for a \ntropical tornado. These tornadoes are very weak and brief. Overall \nQPF continues to be between 0.75-1.5 inches. LPMMs show weak signals \nfor localized areas of about 2.5 inches. Given very dry antecedent \nconditions, not expecting flooding issues, though some localized \nareas could see minor problems. \n\nWinds will weaken and precip chances will decrease from west to \neast on Sunday as the remnants continue to weaken and get picked up \nby upper troughing. \n\n=== Dry to Start Next Week ===\n\nHigh pressure at the surface and ridging aloft will build into the \nregion on Monday and Tuesday. This will allow for drier and warm \nconditions. Highs will remain above normal in the low-mid 80s. \n\nMoving into the middle of next week, a shortwave moving along upper \nridging will bring a cold front through the region. Then deeper \ntroughing is expected to swing into the central Plains that has the \npotential to develop a strong low pressure system. This system would \nimpact the Ohio Valley late next week and into the weekend.",
